window.NTI=window.NTI||{};window.NTI.registerBodies("docker",{"docker-01-jenkins-architecture":{"chunks":[{"anchor":"jenkins-architecture","text":"--- title: Jenkins Architecture category: platforms status: complete difficulty: beginner prerequisites: - Jenkins Domain README related: - Jenkins Agents - Jenkins Setup --- Jenkins Architecture Jenkins follows a controller–agent topology: the controller schedules work and serves the UI; agents nodes provide executors that actually run builds. 1. What Is It? Picking up from the Jenkins Domain map README.md — we now examine the controller–agent topology that every pipeline, agent, and credential later plugs into. - Controller: holds configuration JENKINS_HOME , the job queue, build history, UI/API, and the credentials store. Runs no or minimal builds itself in production. - Agent node : a machine VM, container, laptop connected to the controller offering executors — numbered slots, each running one build at a time. - Remoting: the communication channel inbound TCP/WebSocket or outbound SSH over which the controller ships work to agents. Inbound = the agent dials out survives NAT and firewalls ; SSH = the controller dials in simpler on flat networks . 2. Diagram 3. Key Directories JENKINS_HOME Path Holds ------ ------- jobs/ Job configs + build history workspace/ Checked-out code pe"},{"anchor":"1-what-is-it","text":"r job rebuildable, deletable plugins/ Installed plugins credentials.xml / secrets/ Encrypted secrets back up securely config.xml Global configuration 4. Sizing Rules - Controller: CPU-light, I/O-sensitive build history, XML — give it fast disk, not 64 cores. - Zero executors on the controller in production of executors: 0 so a runaway build can't wedge the UI. - Scale with agents, preferably ephemeral Docker/Kubernetes cloud so capacity matches queue length. 5. Jenkins in Docker: How It Works One docker run produces three planes inside a single container: - Controller process: Jenkins WAR runs as PID 1 Java inside the container; UI on 8080, agent traffic on 50000 — both published to the host. - State: JENKINS_HOME lives on a named volume, not the container layer — recreating the container loses nothing. - Building images: the mounted socket lets the container's Docker CLI ask the host daemon to build/run sibling containers. Jenkins never runs a daemon itself unless DinD sidecar . - Agents: connect over the published 50000/WebSocket back into the controller; Docker-cloud agents are siblings spawned via the same socket. - Networking: agents reach the controller at host:50000 or conta"},{"anchor":"2-diagram","text":"iner name on a shared network ; webhooks reach host:8080/github-webhook/ . 6. Failure Modes - Controller disk fills with old builds → UI freezes; fix with build discarders + log rotation. - All builds pinned to built-in node → single point of contention; fix with labeled agents. - Clock skew between controller and agents → signed artifacts / webhook validation fails; run NTP everywhere. 7. Interview Notes - Controller vs agent responsibilities; why zero executors on controller. - What JENKINS_HOME contains and what must be backed up jobs, credentials, config — not workspaces . Topology understood — now build one. Continue in Jenkins Setup jenkins-setup.md . Related Topics - Jenkins Setup jenkins-setup.md - Jenkins Agents jenkins-agents.md - CI/CD Pipelines ../../06-ci-cd-pipelines/pipelines.md generic concept"}]},"docker-02-lab-08-jenkins-backup-restore":{"chunks":[{"anchor":"lab-08-jenkins-backup-amp-restore","text":"--- title: \"Lab 08 — Jenkins Backup & Restore\" category: labs status: complete difficulty: advanced prerequisites: - Lab 06 - Jenkins Advanced backup section related: - Jenkins Advanced - Rollback & Recovery --- Lab 08 — Jenkins Backup & Restore Objective Prove disaster recovery: back up JENKINS_HOME , destroy the controller, restore on fresh infra, replay the smoke pipeline. Prerequisites - Lab 06 controller with job history; concepts: Jenkins Advanced ../15-platforms-and-tools/jenkins/jenkins-advanced.md backup section. Architecture Running controller → stop → archive jenkins_home volume → wipe → fresh controller → restore archive → verify jobs, credentials, history → run smoke pipeline. Setup Note one job's last build number and one credential ID — these are what you will verify after restore. Step 1 — Back up Execute Expected Result jenkins-backup.tar.gz exists, timestamped; controller restarts normally. Why Offline backup = consistent snapshot; live copies risk half-written state. Step 2 — Destroy and restore Execute Expected Result Jobs, build history, credentials, and plugins all present; recorded build number matches. Why Backup untested = backup imaginary. This drill is th"},{"anchor":"objective","text":"e quarterly DR practice from the advanced doc. Verification - Job list + history identical check the recorded build number . - Credential ID present and usable. - Smoke pipeline Lab 06's runs green on the restored controller. Failure Scenarios - Plugin version drift: restore on a different image tag — pin jenkins:lts digests for controller + backup. - Permission errors: volume owned by wrong UID — chown -R 1000:1000 on the volume. Cleanup Store one backup off-machine; delete the rest. What This Proved Recovery measured, not assumed. Labs complete — return to the Topic Index ../TOPIC_INDEX.md . Interview Notes - The restore proof: which pipeline you run post-restore and what green actually verifies. - Plugin drift and UID ownership — the two restore failures and their one-line fixes."}]},"docker-03-lab-08-jenkins-backup-restore":{"chunks":[{"anchor":"lab-08-jenkins-backup-amp-restore","text":"{/ MIGRATED from: Jenkins/node-multi-branch/16-labs/08-jenkins-backup-restore.md v2.0, 2026-09-01 Course metadata: course=cicd difficulty=Advanced time=60min lab_env=Docker volumes prereqs=lab-06 Outcomes: back up JENKINS_HOME; destroy and restore; replay pipelines Tags: lab, backup, dr Path: labs /} {/ icon: jenkins /} Lab 08 — Jenkins Backup & Restore Objective Prove disaster recovery: back up JENKINS_HOME , destroy the controller, restore on fresh infra, replay the smoke pipeline. Prerequisites - Lab 06 controller with job history; concepts: Jenkins Advanced /courses/jenkins/advanced/ backup section. Architecture Running controller → stop → archive jenkins_home volume → wipe → fresh controller → restore archive → verify jobs, credentials, history → run smoke pipeline. Setup Note one job's last build number and one credential ID — these are what you will verify after restore. Step 1 — Back up Execute Expected Result jenkins-backup.tar.gz exists, timestamped; controller restarts normally. Why Offline backup = consistent snapshot; live copies risk half-written state. Step 2 — Destroy and restore Execute Expected Result Jobs, build history, credentials, and plugins all present; reco"},{"anchor":"objective","text":"rded build number matches. Why Backup untested = backup imaginary. This drill is the quarterly DR practice from the advanced doc. Verification - Job list + history identical check the recorded build number . - Credential ID present and usable. - Smoke pipeline Lab 06's runs green on the restored controller. Failure Scenarios - Plugin version drift: restore on a different image tag — pin jenkins:lts digests for controller + backup. - Permission errors: volume owned by wrong UID — chown -R 1000:1000 on the volume. Cleanup Store one backup off-machine; delete the rest. What This Proved Recovery measured, not assumed. Labs complete. {/ Source closing line referenced TOPIC_INDEX labs index — migrates later with /labs . /}"}]},"docker-04-jenkins-architecture":{"chunks":[{"anchor":"jenkins-architecture","text":"{/ MIGRATED from: Jenkins/node-multi-branch/15-platforms-and-tools/jenkins/jenkins-architecture.md v2.0, 2026-09-01 Course metadata: course=jenkins difficulty=Beginner time=25min prereqs=jenkins-domain Outcomes: explain controller scheduling; choose agent topology; secure remoting Tags: jenkins, architecture Path: jenkins-core /} {/ icon: jenkins /} Jenkins Architecture Jenkins follows a controller–agent topology: the controller schedules work and serves the UI; agents nodes provide executors that actually run builds. 1. What Is It? Picking up from the Jenkins Domain map /courses/jenkins/ — we now examine the controller–agent topology that every pipeline, agent, and credential later plugs into. - Controller: holds configuration JENKINS_HOME , the job queue, build history, UI/API, and the credentials store. Runs no or minimal builds itself in production. - Agent node : a machine VM, container, laptop connected to the controller offering executors — numbered slots, each running one build at a time. - Remoting: the communication channel inbound TCP/WebSocket or outbound SSH over which the controller ships work to agents. Inbound = the agent dials out survives NAT and firewalls ; SSH ="},{"anchor":"1-what-is-it","text":" the controller dials in simpler on flat networks . 2. Diagram 3. Key Directories JENKINS_HOME Path Holds ------ ------- jobs/ Job configs + build history workspace/ Checked-out code per job rebuildable, deletable plugins/ Installed plugins credentials.xml / secrets/ Encrypted secrets back up securely config.xml Global configuration 4. Sizing Rules - Controller: CPU-light, I/O-sensitive build history, XML — give it fast disk, not 64 cores. - Zero executors on the controller in production of executors: 0 so a runaway build can't wedge the UI. - Scale with agents, preferably ephemeral Docker/Kubernetes cloud so capacity matches queue length. {/ icon: docker /} 5. Jenkins in Docker: How It Works One docker run produces three planes inside a single container: - Controller process: Jenkins WAR runs as PID 1 Java inside the container; UI on 8080, agent traffic on 50000 — both published to the host. - State: JENKINS_HOME lives on a named volume, not the container layer — recreating the container loses nothing. - Building images: the mounted socket lets the container's Docker CLI ask the host daemon to build/run sibling containers. Jenkins never runs a daemon itself unless DinD sidecar . -"},{"anchor":"2-diagram","text":" Agents: connect over the published 50000/WebSocket back into the controller; Docker-cloud agents are siblings spawned via the same socket. - Networking: agents reach the controller at host:50000 or container name on a shared network ; webhooks reach host:8080/github-webhook/ . 6. Failure Modes - Controller disk fills with old builds → UI freezes; fix with build discarders + log rotation. - All builds pinned to built-in node → single point of contention; fix with labeled agents. - Clock skew between controller and agents → signed artifacts / webhook validation fails; run NTP everywhere. 7. Interview Notes - Controller vs agent responsibilities; why zero executors on controller. - What JENKINS_HOME contains and what must be backed up jobs, credentials, config — not workspaces . Topology understood — now build one. Continue in Jenkins Setup /courses/jenkins/setup/ . Related Topics - Jenkins Setup /courses/jenkins/setup/ - Jenkins Agents /courses/jenkins/agents/ - CI/CD Pipelines /courses/cicd/pipelines/ generic concept"}]},"docker-05-jenkins-br-span-setup-span":{"chunks":[{"anchor":"jenkinssetup","text":"CI/CD Engineering — PDF 09: Jenkins Setup CI/CD SYSTEMS &nbsp;·&nbsp; ENGINEERING MANUAL &nbsp;·&nbsp; PDF 09 JENKINS SETUP One container, one volume, one socket DOCKER INSTALL · UNLOCK AGENTS · MULTIBRANCH SEVEN STEPS TO GREEN RUN pinned image + volume UNLOCK admin password + plugins CONNECT labeled agent online BUILD multibranch goes green 01 / 05 · INSTALLATION &amp; FIRST RUN Nabawy CI/CD Systems &nbsp; Chapter 09 Install Run · Unlock · Smoke 09 SETUP Picking up from Architecture , where the topology was defined — we now run it : one container, one volume, one socket. 01 / Run &amp; Unlock $ docker volume create jenkins_home $ docker run -d --name jenkins \\ &nbsp;&nbsp;-p 8080:8080 -p 50000:50000 \\ &nbsp;&nbsp;-v jenkins_home:/var/jenkins_home \\ &nbsp;&nbsp;-v /var/run/docker.sock:/var/run/docker.sock \\ &nbsp;&nbsp;jenkins/jenkins:2.516-lts-jdk17 $ docker exec jenkins cat /var/jenkins_home/secrets/initialAdminPassword Open localhost:8080 → paste the password → install suggested plugins → create admin user → set built-in executors to 0 . CI/CD Engineering Manual CH 09 02 / 05 CI/CD Systems &nbsp; Chapter 09 Platforms Choices · Windows · Smoke 02 / Where to Run It Method When Doc"},{"anchor":"install","text":"ker image Default: dev, training, labs OS package Long-lived on-prem server Kubernetes Helm Production, ephemeral agents WINDOWS — native msi works, but prefer Docker Desktop WSL2 backend : same command, Linux-normal paths. Keep a Windows agent label windows only for .NET/MSBuild workloads. Watch bat vs sh , CRLF, and firewall ports 8080/50000. 03 / Smoke Test New Item → Pipeline → hello → inline script: pipeline { agent any { stage 'Hello' { echo 'alive' } } } Build Now → green ball = controller + executors + workspace work CI/CD Engineering Manual CH 09 03 / 05 CI/CD Systems &nbsp; Chapter 09 Seven Steps Run · Use · Verify 04 / Run &amp; Use, End to End 1 · Run — container + volume + socket above . 2 · Unlock — password, suggested plugins, admin user. 3 · Lock controller — built-in executors = 0. 4 · Agent — new node linux-1 , labels linux docker , idle in Executor Status. 5 · Multibranch job — repo URL + credentials; Jenkinsfile discovered per branch. 6 · Webhook — /github-webhook/ ; push → build in seconds. 7 · Verify loop — green → staging → approve → production. Break it on purpose once. CI/CD Engineering Manual CH 09 04 / 05 CI/CD Systems &nbsp; Chapter 09 Harden &amp; Hando"},{"anchor":"01-run-amp-unlock","text":"ff Checklist · Failures 05 / Production Checklist Persistent, backed-up JENKINS_HOME test restore . Pinned image + plugin versions; deliberate upgrades. TLS reverse proxy + auth matrix from day one. Lost data on recreate = volume wasn't mounted. Port 50000 blocked = agents can't connect. 06 / Handoff Server running and first job green — now write real pipelines. Continue in PDF 10 · Jenkins Pipelines . SOURCE: 15-platforms-and-tools/jenkins/jenkins-setup.md · Print: Ctrl/Cmd+P → Save as PDF → Background graphics on → A4. CI/CD Engineering Manual CH 09 05 / 05"}]},"docker-06-docker-br-span-build-amp-push-span":{"chunks":[{"anchor":"dockerbuild-amp-push","text":"Lab 02 — Docker Build & Push Jenkins Labs CI/CD Systems &nbsp;·&nbsp; Pipeline Control Room &nbsp;·&nbsp; Jenkins Labs LAB 02 DOCKER BUILD &amp; PUSH From repository to registry to runtime. Jenkins pulls source, builds an immutable image, authenticates with vaulted credentials, tags by build number, pushes to Docker Hub, and optionally runs the same artifact inside a FLOCI simulated AWS environment. IMAGE_NAME:LOCAL → TAG:BUILD-N → PUSH → FLOCI DEPLOY &nbsp;·&nbsp; withCredentials · BUILD_NUMBER 01 — ENVIRONMENT SETUP Load config · env IMAGE_NAME / IMAGE_TAG 02 — CODE CHECKOUT checkout scm · hello.py + Dockerfile + Jenkinsfile 03 — DOCKER BUILD docker build -t app:local . 04 — AUTHENTICATION withCredentials · docker login --password-stdin 05 — IMAGE TAGGING docker tag app:local → myusername/app:build-N 06 — IMAGE PUSH docker push myusername/app:build-N → Docker Hub 07 — APPLICATION DEPLOYMENT when DEPLOY_TO_FLOCI → run hello.py in FLOCI simulation 7 Stages 1 Immutable Tag / Build 0 Hardcoded Secrets 2 Flows: Core + Extended LAB 02 · DOCKER BUILD · PUSH · FLOCI &nbsp;·&nbsp; 01 / 07 Nabawy CI/CD Systems &nbsp; Lab 02 — Docker Build &amp; Push Architecture Core build-push & extended "},{"anchor":"architecture","text":"7-stage with FLOCI 01 / Two Views of the Same Pipeline The core flow is four operations Build → Login &amp; Tag → Push fed by a Git source and the Jenkins credentials store. The extended flow is the seven numbered stages that Jenkins actually executes, ending with an optional deployment into FLOCI's simulated AWS — not real cloud. Core Build-Push Flow Image 2 Git Source Repository hello.py · requirements.txt · Dockerfile · Jenkinsfile ↓ git clone Jenkins CI/CD Pipeline Orchestrator Pull Code — git clone repo_url Build Setup — tool configure docker, build-env Docker Build — docker build -t app:local Login &amp; Tag — docker login + docker tag app:local → build-123 Docker Push — docker push myusername/app:build-123 ↓ upload Docker Hub Registry User Repositories · Image Tiers Jenkins Credentials Store — fetch dockerhub-creds → feeds Login &amp; Tag Extended 7-Stage Flow with FLOCI Image 3 GitHub Repository webhook trigger → 01 Environment Setup — Load Env 02 Code Checkout — Retrieve Repo 03 Docker Build — v${BUILD_NUMBER} 04 Authentication — Login to Registry 05 Image Tagging — Apply Build Tag 06 Image Push — to Registry 07 Application Deployment — Run App ─→ FLOCI AWS Simulation Prov"},{"anchor":"01-two-views-of-the-same-pipeline","text":"ider: aws → floci chip/container icon → running hello.py inside simulation 02 / Stage Lane — Where Each Stage Runs JENKINS 01 Setup · 02 Checkout · 03 Build · 04 Auth · 05 Tag · 06 Push controller / agent REGISTRY 06 Push lands → myusername/app:build-N visible on Docker Hub Docker Hub FLOCI 07 Deploy — same image runs as simulated container simulated CONTINUITY Lab 01 proved commit → webhook → build . This lab answers the next question: what does the build produce? An immutable, tagged image that any environment — including FLOCI — can run without rebuilding. CI/CD Engineering Manual LAB 02 · ARCHITECTURE 02 / 07 Labs &nbsp; Lab 02 — Docker Pre-Flight & Setup Step 0 — before you mutate anything 03 / Step 0 — Pre-Flight Check Do this entire section before Step 1 so the pipeline builds end-to-end without stalling mid-setup. Check Command / Location Decision Tools curl -s -o /dev/null -w \"%{http_code}\" http://localhost:8080 docker --version · docker info · git --version Jenkins: Docker Pipeline + Credentials Binding plugins installed Confirm Jenkins user can run docker group / socket / DiD . Check plugin manager. Credentials Manage Jenkins → Credentials → search for Docker Hub credent"},{"anchor":"core-build-push-flow-image-2","text":"ial Docker Hub: Account Settings → Security → New Access Token Reuse if present. If none: need username + access token not password . Repo / Job hello.py · requirements.txt · Dockerfile · Jenkinsfile exist? Pipeline job exists? Docker Hub namespace? Webhook configured? Note repo URL/branch, image name myusername/app , visibility public/private . FLOCI FLOCI endpoint / simulation config documented? Only if stage 07 in scope — get interface/command from user in Step 0.4. ASK IN ONE BATCH — BEFORE STEP 1 Only ask for what is genuinely missing after the checks: Docker Hub username + access token if no Jenkins credential , image namespace + tagging convention build-${BUILD_NUMBER} , FLOCI in scope? + endpoint , Git URL/branch . Then proceed uninterrupted. 04 / Steps 1–2 — Repository &amp; Credentials 01 — Prepare the Repository Ensure the repo contains hello.py · requirements.txt · Dockerfile · Jenkinsfile built in Step 3 . Commit missing pieces and push. Skip/reuse anything already present. 02 — Add Docker Hub Credentials to Jenkins Manage Jenkins → Credentials → Add Kind: Username with password Username = Docker Hub user Password = access token not account password ID = dockerhub-cred"},{"anchor":"extended-7-stage-flow-with-floci-image-3","text":"s NEVER — Hardcoded in Jenkinsfile sh 'docker login -u myuser -p s3cret123' ← in Git history forever Secret visible in SCM, logs, and diffs. Revoking means rewriting history. ALWAYS — withCredentials Binding withCredentials usernamePassword credentialsId : 'dockerhub-creds', usernameVariable: 'DOCKER_USER', passwordVariable: 'DOCKER_PASS' { sh 'echo $DOCKER_PASS docker login -u $DOCKER_USER --password-stdin' } Injected at runtime, masked in logs, never stored in Git. Token scoped &amp; revocable. verify tools before you start $ docker --version Docker version 26.x, build xxxxx $ docker info head -5 Server: Docker Engine — check Jenkins user can run docker commands $ curl -s -o /dev/null -w \"%{http_code}\" http://localhost:8080 200 ← Jenkins reachable CI/CD Engineering Manual LAB 02 · PRE-FLIGHT 03 / 07 Labs &nbsp; Lab 02 — Docker Pipeline as Code Step 3 — Jenkinsfile · seven stages 05 / Full Jenkinsfile — Seven Stages Structure matches Image 3's numbered flow. IMAGE_TAG = build-${env.BUILD_NUMBER} makes every build immutable. The last stage is conditional — only runs when DEPLOY_TO_FLOCI == true . pipeline { agent any environment { IMAGE_NAME = \"myusername/app\" IMAGE_TAG = \"build-${"},{"anchor":"02-stage-lane-where-each-stage-runs","text":"env.BUILD_NUMBER}\" } stages { stage 'Environment Setup' { steps { echo 'Loading environment/config...' } } stage 'Code Checkout' { steps { checkout scm } } stage 'Docker Build' { steps { sh \"docker build -t ${IMAGE_NAME}:local .\" } } stage 'Authentication' { steps { withCredentials usernamePassword credentialsId: 'dockerhub-creds', usernameVariable: 'DOCKER_USER', passwordVariable: 'DOCKER_PASS' { sh 'echo $DOCKER_PASS docker login -u $DOCKER_USER --password-stdin' } } } stage 'Image Tagging' { steps { sh \"docker tag ${IMAGE_NAME}:local ${IMAGE_NAME}:${IMAGE_TAG}\" } } stage 'Image Push' { steps { sh \"docker push ${IMAGE_NAME}:${IMAGE_TAG}\" } } stage 'Application Deployment' { when { expression { return params.DEPLOY_TO_FLOCI == true } } steps { // FLOCI interface depends on Step 0.4 — fill per user-supplied details echo \"Deploying ${IMAGE_NAME}:${IMAGE_TAG} into FLOCI simulation...\" } } } } 06 / Pipeline Diagram 01 ENV SETUP load config 02 CHECKOUT checkout scm 03 BUILD docker build 04 AUTH withCredentials 05 TAG build-N 06 PUSH docker push 07 DEPLOY FLOCI · when ARTIFACT — myusername/app:build-123 Built once from commit SHA · tagged immutably · pulled everywhere without rebuild PU"},{"anchor":"continuity","text":"RPLE · ARTIFACT withCredentials PATTERN usernamePassword credentialsId: 'dockerhub-creds' injects two env vars at runtime. Jenkins masks $DOCKER_PASS in console output . Using --password-stdin avoids the password appearing in process args. Prefer a scoped access token over the account password — tokens are revokable per purpose. CI/CD Engineering Manual LAB 02 · JENKINSFILE 04 / 07 Labs &nbsp; Lab 02 — Docker Run & Validate Steps 4–5 — trigger · push · prove 07 / Step 4 — Configure the Pipeline Job JOB — Create or reuse a Pipeline job pointing at the Git repo. Use Pipeline script from SCM so it reads the Jenkinsfile directly from the repo. Set up the GitHub webhook trigger companion task so pushes trigger this pipeline automatically. 08 / Step 5 — Run and Validate End-to-End Trigger via webhook push or manually. Confirm each stage completes in order. trigger — push a commit or click Build Now $ git commit --allow-empty -m \"trigger docker build\" && git push expected stage progression in Jenkins Blue Ocean / Console 01 Environment Setup ● PASSED Loading environment/config... 02 Code Checkout ● PASSED checkout scm — commit a1b2c3d 03 Docker Build ● PASSED Successfully built 7f91c2 · t"},{"anchor":"pre-flight-amp-setup","text":"agged app:local 04 Authentication ● PASSED Login Succeeded password masked 05 Image Tagging ● PASSED tagged myusername/app:build-42 06 Image Push ● PASSED build-42: digest: sha256:… size: 1234 07 Application Deploy ○ SKIPPED when DEPLOY_TO_FLOCI == false with DEPLOY_TO_FLOCI == true → 07 Application Deploy ● PASSED Deploying myusername/app:build-42 into FLOCI... VERIFY — Three proofs Docker Hub: myusername/app:build-N appears under the expected repository. ● PUSHED Increment: Push a second commit → new tag build-N+1 — BUILD_NUMBER auto-increments. ● IMMUTABLE FLOCI if in scope : Simulation reflects the running hello.py container. ● DEPLOYED 09 / Status Indicators State Meaning Where to See It BUILDING docker build in progress Agent executor · Docker daemon log · stage view PASSED Stage succeeded Console Output — green stage header PUSHED Image visible on Docker Hub docker pull myusername/app:build-N locally WAITING Auth pending / tag pending Stage waiting for prior stage SKIPPED Stage 07 when FLOCI not in scope when { DEPLOY_TO_FLOCI == false } FAILED Login denied / push rejected / build error Console red · Docker Hub 401/403 · check credential ID + token scope PROOF — The same com"},{"anchor":"03-step-0-pre-flight-check","text":"mit SHA was built once, tagged immutably as build-N , and pushed as a reusable artifact. The registry is the handoff. Any environment pulls the same bytes . ● ARTIFACT PROVED CI/CD Engineering Manual LAB 02 · VALIDATE 05 / 07 Labs &nbsp; Lab 02 — Docker Gotchas & Failure Modes What breaks and how to recover 10 / Gotchas GOTCHA — NEVER HARDCODE THE SECRET Putting the Docker Hub password/token directly in the Jenkinsfile commits it to Git history forever. Always use withCredentials usernamePassword credentialsId: 'dockerhub-creds', ... with --password-stdin . The token is masked as in logs. GOTCHA — ACCESS TOKEN, NOT ACCOUNT PASSWORD Docker Hub access tokens are scoped and revokable per purpose. Generate under Account Settings → Security → New Access Token . If the token leaks you revoke one token, not the whole account. The stored Jenkins credential's password field holds the token. GOTCHA — DOCKER-IN-DOCKER vs SOCKET MOUNT Don't assume the execution model. Run docker info on the actual Jenkins agent to confirm whether it uses a mounted /var/run/docker.sock or Docker-in-Docker. Wrong assumption → Cannot connect to the Docker daemon at build time. Decide in Step 0. GOTCHA — FLOCI IS "},{"anchor":"ask-in-one-batch-before-step-1","text":"NOT REAL AWS The Application Deployment stage is FLOCI-specific. Real AWS CLI/ECR docs do not apply. Get the actual FLOCI interface and deploy command from the user in Step 0.4 — don't guess at syntax. Stage 07 stays when { DEPLOY_TO_FLOCI == true } so the pipeline passes without it when out of scope. GOTCHA — REPO VISIBILITY &amp; FIRST PUSH Docker Hub auto-creates the repository on first push if the account allows. Confirm expected visibility public vs private before pushing. A private repo with a public assumption breaks downstream pulls. 11 / Failure → Recovery Map Failure Where to Look Fix docker login denied Stage 04 console: 401 Unauthorized Verify credential ID = dockerhub-creds , token not expired, scope includes push docker push 403 Stage 06 console: denied: requested access Namespace mismatch myusername must match Docker Hub account or repo is private without auth Cannot connect to daemon Stage 03: docker build fails immediately Mount socket / run DiD; add Jenkins user to docker group; check docker info Dockerfile not found Stage 03: failed to process Dockerfile Confirm Dockerfile at repo root; checkout scm pulled correct branch Build tag already exists Stage 05/06: push"},{"anchor":"04-steps-1-2-repository-amp-credentials","text":" overwrites or is rejected Always use build-${BUILD_NUMBER} — never latest alone for CI FLOCI deploy fails Stage 07 console Validate FLOCI endpoint/creds from Step 0.4; confirm conditional when logic quick diagnostics when a stage fails $ docker pull myusername/app:build-42 ← proves registry + tag exist $ echo $DOCKER_PASS docker login -u $DOCKER_USER --password-stdin ← test creds locally $ docker info && docker --version ← confirm execution model $ curl -s http://localhost:8080 head ← Jenkins reachable? CI/CD Engineering Manual LAB 02 · GOTCHAS 06 / 07 CI/CD Systems &nbsp; Lab 02 — Docker Proof & System Map What this lab proved · where it sits 12 / Proof PROVED — Jenkins produced a Docker image from source, authenticated via vaulted credentials without hardcoding secrets, tagged it immutably with build-${BUILD_NUMBER} , pushed it to Docker Hub, and — when in scope — ran the same artifact inside FLOCI. A second commit incremented the tag automatically. ● DOCKER BUILD &amp; PUSH PROVED ● FLOCI DEPLOY PROVED REGISTRY AS HANDOFF Source → Build → Tag → Push → Pull → Run. Every environment runs the same digest , not a rebuild. REGISTRY 13 / Interview Checks WHY withCredentials + --passw"},{"anchor":"01-prepare-the-repository","text":"ord-stdin? Injects the secret as env vars at runtime, masks it in logs , avoids it appearing in process args or Git history. --password-stdin keeps it out of ps . WHY build-${BUILD_NUMBER} NOT latest? latest is mutable and unrepeatable. build-N is immutable — you can roll back to any N, and every deploy is traceable to one Jenkins build. WHY IS STAGE 07 CONDITIONAL? FLOCI is a simulation, not always in scope. when { DEPLOY_TO_FLOCI == true } lets the same Jenkinsfile cover both flows — pipeline passes with 6 stages when deployment is out of scope, 7 when in. WHAT DOES A GREEN BUILD NOT PROVE? It proves the checks in that pipeline passed — build, login, push. It doesn't prove the app works in production. Registry presence ≠ runtime correctness. 14 / Lab 02 in the Control Room Loop SOURCE GitHub push · hello.py + Dockerfile + Jenkinsfile PIPELINE Jenkins orchestrator · 7 stages · withCredentials ARTIFACT myusername/app:build-N · immutable image REGISTRY Docker Hub · User Repositories · Image Tiers DEPLOY FLOCI simulation when in scope → running hello.py OBSERVE stage view · console · Docker Hub · FLOCI state └────────────→ FEEDBACK → NEXT COMMIT → BUILD-N+1 WHAT COMES NEXT Lab 02's a"},{"anchor":"02-add-docker-hub-credentials-to-jenkins","text":"rtifact is the unit that later labs multiply and reuse. Lab 03 builds it per branch via multibranch discovery. Lab 04 extracts shared logic into a versioned library. Lab 05 applies the same gated pipeline to infrastructure on FLOCI. SOURCE: jenkins labs/Jenkins_Docker_Build_Push_DockerHub_FLOCI_Deploy.md &nbsp;·&nbsp; Print: Ctrl/Cmd+P → Save as PDF → Background graphics ON → A4 &nbsp;·&nbsp; Icons: docker · jenkins · package · database · rocket · github · shield. CI/CD Engineering Manual LAB 02 · DOCKER BUILD &amp; PUSH 07 / 07"}]},"docker-07-command-cheatsheet":{"chunks":[{"anchor":"command-cheatsheet","text":"--- title: Command Cheatsheet category: reference status: complete difficulty: beginner prerequisites: related: - Git, Branching & Pull Requests - CI/CD Pipelines --- Command Cheatsheet Every command used across the labs and guides, grouped by tool. Copy-paste ready; details live in the linked chapters. Git See Git, Branching & Pull Requests ../01-source-control/git-branching-pull-requests.md . npm / Builds See Build Systems ../03-build-systems/build-systems.md . Docker / GHCR See Artifact Management ../05-artifacts-and-packaging/artifact-management.md . Jenkins Compose lab See Lab 06 ../16-labs/06-jenkins-controller.md , Lab 08 ../16-labs/08-jenkins-backup-restore.md . Jenkins backup See Jenkins Advanced ../15-platforms-and-tools/jenkins/jenkins-advanced.md . GitHub Actions snippets See GitHub Actions ../15-platforms-and-tools/github-actions.md . Interview Notes - Reproduce any pipeline in this file from memory: trigger, build, gate, promote, rollback. - Which snippet pins versions — and what breaks first when nothing is pinned. - Where the approval gate lives in each platform's snippet, and what happens without it. Related Topics - Topic Index ../TOPIC_INDEX.md - Glossary ../GLOS"},{"anchor":"git","text":"SARY.md"}]},"docker-08-jenkins-br-span-agents-span":{"chunks":[{"anchor":"jenkinsagents","text":"CI/CD Engineering — PDF 12: Jenkins Agents CI/CD SYSTEMS &nbsp;·&nbsp; ENGINEERING MANUAL &nbsp;·&nbsp; PDF 12 JENKINS AGENTS Labels select them — executors run on them PERMANENT · DOCKER · KUBERNETES LABELS · EXECUTORS EPHEMERAL BY DEFAULT PERMANENT SSH/inbound VMs DOCKER CLOUD container per build KUBERNETES pod per build 01 / 03 · EXECUTION FLEET Nabawy CI/CD Systems &nbsp; Chapter 12 Fleet Types · Labels · Wiring 12 AGENTS Picking up from Groovy — pipelines written, language in hand — we now provision the machines that execute them . 01 / Three Kinds Type Lifecycle Best for Permanent Long-lived VM Special HW, Windows/GPU Docker cloud Container per build Default Linux — clean every run Kubernetes Pod per build Elastic, bursty CI 02 / Labels &amp; Executors agent { label 'linux && docker' } // must have BOTH agent { docker { image 'node:20' } } // ephemeral container agent none // each stage picks LABEL = capability tag matched by jobs. EXECUTOR = one build slot ~1 per 1–2 cores . Wrong labels = builds queue forever. CI/CD Engineering Manual CH 12 02 / 03 CI/CD Systems &nbsp; Chapter 12 Wiring Connection · Trust · Next 03 / Connection &amp; Trust Inbound — agent dials out via WebS"},{"anchor":"fleet","text":"ocket; survives NAT, needs port 50000. SSH — controller dials in; simple on flat networks, needs port 22 + credential. Ephemeral wins — pristine container per run kills \"works on my machine\" and tool drift. TRUST BOUNDARY Never run untrusted fork PRs on permanent agents holding deploy credentials. Forks get isolated, credential-free agents — a malicious workflow otherwise owns your box. Label typo → queue forever; check Executor Status. Too many executors per CPU → starvation. 04 / Handoff Agents run the work — next they need secrets. Continue in PDF 13 · Jenkins Credentials . SOURCE: 15-platforms-and-tools/jenkins/jenkins-agents.md · Print: Ctrl/Cmd+P → Save as PDF → Background graphics on → A4. CI/CD Engineering Manual CH 12 03 / 03"}]},"docker-09-jenkins-agents":{"chunks":[{"anchor":"jenkins-agents","text":"--- title: Jenkins Agents category: platforms status: complete difficulty: intermediate prerequisites: - Jenkins Architecture - Jenkins Pipelines related: - Jenkins Security --- Jenkins Agents Agents are Jenkins's word for runners: machines offering executors to the controller, selected by labels . Prefer ephemeral agents that live only for one build. 1. Types Picking up from the Groovy Cheatsheet jenkins-groovy-cheatsheet.md — pipelines written, language in hand — we now provision the machines that execute them: agents, labels, executors. Type Lifecycle Best for ------ ----------- ---------- Permanent SSH/inbound Long-lived VM Special hardware, Windows/GPU builders Docker cloud Container per build, destroyed after Default Linux builds — clean env every time Kubernetes cloud Pod per build Elastic, bursty CI on existing clusters 2. Labels & Executors - Label: capability tag linux , windows , gpu , docker matched by agent { label ... } . - Executor: one build slot; a 4-core agent typically offers 2–4 executors. 3. Minimal Docker-Agent Pipeline Each run gets a pristine container — \"works on my machine\" dies here. 4. Connection Methods - Inbound agent → controller : agent initiates via"},{"anchor":"1-types","text":" WebSocket/JNLP; survives NAT, needs port 50000 or WebSocket endpoint. - SSH controller → agent : controller SSHes in; simple on flat networks, needs credential + open port 22. 5. Failure Modes - Label typo lable 'linx' → build waits forever in queue; check Build Executor Status . - Stale permanent agents tool drift → flaky builds; prefer ephemeral or reimage regularly. - Too many executors per CPU → builds starve each other; ~1 executor per 1–2 cores. 6. Interview Notes - Inbound vs SSH connection: which side initiates, and which survives NAT without opening inbound ports. - Why ephemeral agents beat permanent ones for reproducibility — what \"tool drift\" does to flaky-build rates. - Label expressions vs executor counts: what a build stuck in queue almost always means. Agents run the work — next they need secrets. Continue in Jenkins Credentials jenkins-credentials.md . Related Topics - Jenkins Architecture jenkins-architecture.md - Jenkins Pipelines jenkins-pipelines.md"}]},"docker-10-dockerfile":{"chunks":[{"text":"FROM python:3.12-slim WORKDIR /app COPY hello.py . CMD [\"python\", \"hello.py\"]"}]},"docker-11-jenkinsfile":{"chunks":[{"text":"pipeline { agent any environment { DOCKER_IMAGE = 'yourusername/myapp' // TODO: replace with your Docker Hub repo IMAGE_TAG = \"build-${env.BUILD_NUMBER}\" } stages { stage('Checkout') { steps { checkout scm } } stage('Build') { steps { sh 'docker build -t $DOCKER_IMAGE:$IMAGE_TAG .' } } stage('Login & Push') { steps { withCredentials([usernamePassword( credentialsId: 'dockerhub-credentials', usernameVariable: 'U', passwordVariable: 'P')]) { sh 'echo \"$P\" | docker login -u \"$U\" --password-stdin' sh 'docker push $DOCKER_IMAGE:$IMAGE_TAG' sh 'docker tag $DOCKER_IMAGE:$IMAGE_TAG $DOCKER_IMAGE:latest && docker push $DOCKER_IMAGE:latest' } } } stage('Smoke test') { steps { sh 'docker run --rm $DOCKER_IMAGE:$IMAGE_TAG' } } } post { always { sh 'docker rmi $DOCKER_IMAGE:$IMAGE_TAG || true' sh 'docker logout || true' } } }"}]},"docker-12-hello-py":{"chunks":[{"text":"print(\"Hello from Jenkins + Docker Hub!\", flush=True)"}]},"docker-13-jenkinsfile":{"chunks":[{"text":"pipeline { agent any environment { // Step 1: Environment Setup DOCKER_CREDS_ID = \"docker-hub-creds\" DOCKER_IMAGE = \"eslamnabawy/hello-app\" IMAGE_TAG = \"v${env.BUILD_NUMBER}\" FLOCI_ENDPOINT = \"http://floci-simulator-api:4566\" } stages { stage(\"Step 2: Code Checkout\") { steps { echo \"Checking out source repository...\" checkout scm } } stage(\"Step 3: Docker Build\") { steps { echo \"Building Docker Image...\" script { sh \"docker build -t ${DOCKER_IMAGE}:latest .\" } } } stage(\"Step 4 & 5: Auth & Image Tagging\") { steps { echo \"Authenticating and applying build tag...\" script { sh \"docker tag ${DOCKER_IMAGE}:latest ${DOCKER_IMAGE}:${IMAGE_TAG}\" withCredentials([usernamePassword(credentialsId: DOCKER_CREDS_ID, passwordVariable: \"DOCKER_PASS\", usernameVariable: \"DOCKER_USER\")]) { sh \"echo $DOCKER_PASS | docker login -u $DOCKER_USER --password-stdin\" } } } } stage(\"Step 6: Image Push\") { steps { echo \"Pushing image tag: ${IMAGE_TAG} to registry...\" script { sh \"docker push ${DOCKER_IMAGE}:${IMAGE_TAG}\" sh \"docker push ${DOCKER_IMAGE}:latest\" } } } stage(\"Step 7: Application Deployment (Floci)\") { steps { echo \"Deploying to Floci Simulated AWS Environment...\" script { // Trigger simulated dep"},{"text":"loyment via Floci API sh \"\"\" curl -X POST ${FLOCI_ENDPOINT}/deploy -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" -d '{\"image\": \"${DOCKER_IMAGE}:${IMAGE_TAG}\", \"service\": \"hello-app\"}' \"\"\" } } } } post { always { echo \"Pipeline execution complete. Cleaning up local workspace.\" sh \"docker logout || true\" } } }"}]},"docker-14-jenkinsfile":{"chunks":[{"text":"pipeline { agent any environment { DOCKERHUB_CREDENTIALS = credentials('docker-hub-creds') DOCKER_IMAGE = \"eslamnabawy/hello-app\" IMAGE_TAG = \"build-${BUILD_NUMBER}\" DOCKER_BIN = \"/usr/local/bin/docker\" } stages { stage('Checkout Repo') { steps { checkout scm } } stage('Build Docker Image') { steps { script { echo \"Building Docker Image...\"; sh \"${DOCKER_BIN} build -t ${DOCKER_IMAGE}:${IMAGE_TAG} .\" } } } stage('Login to Docker Hub') { steps { script { echo \"Logging into Docker Hub...\"; sh \"echo \\$DOCKERHUB_CREDENTIALS_PSW | ${DOCKER_BIN} login -u \\$DOCKERHUB_CREDENTIALS_USR --password-stdin\" } } } stage('Push Image') { steps { script { echo \"Pushing image to Docker Hub...\"; sh \"${DOCKER_BIN} push ${DOCKER_IMAGE}:${IMAGE_TAG}\"; sh \"${DOCKER_BIN} tag ${DOCKER_IMAGE}:${IMAGE_TAG} ${DOCKER_IMAGE}:latest\"; sh \"${DOCKER_BIN} push ${DOCKER_IMAGE}:latest\" } } } } post { always { sh \"${DOCKER_BIN} rmi ${DOCKER_IMAGE}:${IMAGE_TAG} || true\" sh \"${DOCKER_BIN} rmi ${DOCKER_IMAGE}:latest || true\" sh \"${DOCKER_BIN} logout || true\" } } }"}]},"docker-15-check-if-we-can-push-try-login-check":{"chunks":[{"anchor":"check-if-we-can-push-try-login-check","text":"/usr/bin/env bash set -e PROJECT=/mnt/c/Users/eslam/OneDrive/Desktop/Jenkins cd \"$PROJECT\" DOCKER_IMAGE=\"eslamnabawy/hello-app\" BUILD_NUMBER=15 IMAGE_TAG=\"v${BUILD_NUMBER}\" echo \"=== STAGE 1: Environment Setup ===\" echo \"DOCKER_IMAGE=$DOCKER_IMAGE\" echo \"IMAGE_TAG=$IMAGE_TAG\" echo \"BUILD_NUMBER=$BUILD_NUMBER\" echo \"FLOCI_ENDPOINT=http://floci-simulator-api:4566\" echo \"\" echo \"=== STAGE 2: Code Checkout ===\" ls -lh echo \"\" echo \"=== STAGE 3: Docker Build eslamnabawy ===\" docker build -t ${DOCKER_IMAGE}:latest . docker images ${DOCKER_IMAGE} --format \"table {{.Repository}}\\t{{.Tag}}\\t{{.Size}}\\t{{.CreatedAt}}\" echo \"\" echo \"=== STAGE 4 & 5: Auth & Image Tagging ===\" docker tag ${DOCKER_IMAGE}:latest ${DOCKER_IMAGE}:${IMAGE_TAG} docker images ${DOCKER_IMAGE} --format \"table {{.Repository}}\\t{{.Tag}}\\t{{.ID}}\" echo \"Jenkins credentials: docker-hub-creds - eslamnabawy verified \" docker exec jenkins grep -A2 \"docker-hub-creds\" /var/jenkins_home/credentials.xml head -n 5 echo \"\" echo \"=== STAGE 6: Image Push ===\" echo \"Attempting docker push requires Docker Hub login ...\" Check if we can push - try login check if docker push ${DOCKER_IMAGE}:${IMAGE_TAG} 2 &1; then echo \"Pushed ${IMAGE_TAG"},{"anchor":"ensure-floci-mock-api-on-4566","text":"}\" docker push ${DOCKER_IMAGE}:latest 2 &1 tail -n 20 else echo \"Push failed - likely not logged in. Run: echo \\$DOCKER_TOKEN docker login -u eslamnabawy --password-stdin\" echo \"Then re-run: docker push ${DOCKER_IMAGE}:${IMAGE_TAG} && docker push ${DOCKER_IMAGE}:latest\" fi echo \"\" echo \"=== STAGE 7: Deployment to Floci ===\" docker rm -f floci-hello-app 2 /dev/null true docker run -d --name floci-hello-app -p 8081:8081 -e PORT=8081 ${DOCKER_IMAGE}:${IMAGE_TAG} sleep 3 docker ps --filter name=floci-hello-app --format \"table {{.Names}}\\t{{.Status}}\\t{{.Ports}}\\t{{.Image}}\" curl -s http://localhost:8081/ echo \"curl failed\" echo \"\" Ensure Floci mock API on 4566 cat /tmp/floci_mock.py /dev/null 2 &1; then pkill -f floci_mock.py 2 /dev/null true nohup python3 /tmp/floci_mock.py /tmp/floci_mock.log 2 &1 & sleep 2 fi echo \"Simulating Jenkinsfile curl to Floci:\" curl -s -X POST http://localhost:4566/deploy -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" -d \"{\\\"image\\\":\\\"${DOCKER_IMAGE}:${IMAGE_TAG}\\\",\\\"service\\\":\\\"hello-app\\\"}\" echo \"\" echo \"\" echo \"=== PIPELINE COMPLETE eslamnabawy ===\" echo \"Verify: curl http://localhost:8081/\" curl -s http://localhost:8081/ echo \"\" echo \"Images:\" docker images grep -"},{"anchor":"ensure-floci-mock-api-on-4566","text":"E \"hello-app REPOSITORY\""}]},"docker-16-jenkins-agents":{"chunks":[{"anchor":"jenkins-agents","text":"{/ MIGRATED from: Jenkins/node-multi-branch/15-platforms-and-tools/jenkins/jenkins-agents.md v2.0, 2026-09-01 Course metadata: course=jenkins difficulty=Intermediate time=30min prereqs=jenkins-architecture Outcomes: select via labels; size executors; run Docker/K8s agents Tags: jenkins, agents Path: jenkins-core /} {/ icon: server /} Jenkins Agents Agents are Jenkins's word for runners: machines offering executors to the controller, selected by labels . Prefer ephemeral agents that live only for one build. 1. Types Picking up from the Groovy Cheatsheet /courses/jenkins/groovy/ — pipelines written, language in hand — we now provision the machines that execute them: agents, labels, executors. Type Lifecycle Best for ------ ----------- ---------- Permanent SSH/inbound Long-lived VM Special hardware, Windows/GPU builders Docker cloud Container per build, destroyed after Default Linux builds — clean env every time Kubernetes cloud Pod per build Elastic, bursty CI on existing clusters 2. Labels & Executors - Label: capability tag linux , windows , gpu , docker matched by agent { label ... } . - Executor: one build slot; a 4-core agent typically offers 2–4 executors. 3. Minimal Docker-Age"},{"anchor":"1-types","text":"nt Pipeline Each run gets a pristine container — \"works on my machine\" dies here. 4. Connection Methods - Inbound agent → controller : agent initiates via WebSocket/JNLP; survives NAT, needs port 50000 or WebSocket endpoint. - SSH controller → agent : controller SSHes in; simple on flat networks, needs credential + open port 22. 5. Failure Modes - Label typo lable 'linx' → build waits forever in queue; check Build Executor Status . - Stale permanent agents tool drift → flaky builds; prefer ephemeral or reimage regularly. - Too many executors per CPU → builds starve each other; ~1 executor per 1–2 cores. Agents run the work — next they need secrets. Continue in Jenkins Credentials /courses/jenkins/credentials/ . Related Topics - Jenkins Architecture /courses/jenkins/architecture/ - Jenkins Pipelines /courses/jenkins/pipelines/"}]},"docker-17-contents-10-pages-10-sections":{"chunks":[{"anchor":"contents-10-pages-10-sections","text":"Command Cheatsheet — CICD BY Nabawy ☰ Contents · 10 CICD BY Nabawy / Command Cheatsheet ⌘K ← Back Home Threads Tools ⛶ ◐ ☰ CONTENTS · 10 PAGES · 10 SECTIONS × Cover — Command Cheatsheet Cover — Table of Contents Cover — Git Cover — npm / Builds Cover — Docker / GHCR Cover — Jenkins Compose lab Cover — Jenkins backup Cover — GitHub Actions snippets Cover — Interview Notes Cover — Related Topics SIGNAL SERIES · REFERENCE Command Cheatsheet Every command used across the labs and guides, grouped by tool. Copy-paste ready; details live in the linked chapters. By Nabawy AUTHOR &amp; COMPILER Git npm / Builds Docker / GHCR Jenkins Compose lab Jenkins backup V4 · 2026 · REFERENCE · verified 2026-09-18 Contents Table of Contents 8 sheets 03 Git 03 04 npm / Builds 04 05 Docker / GHCR 05 06 Jenkins Compose lab 06 07 Jenkins backup 07 08 GitHub Actions snippets 08 09 Interview Notes 09 10 Related Topics 10 How to use this book legend NOTE · context KEY · must retain WATCH OUT · gotcha VERIFIED · check NOTE Read cover to cover once, then use the Contents to jump. Every chapter ends with a 🎓 EXAM box — if you can answer it cold, move on. 📘 Command Cheatsheet · By Nabawy — 2 — Start ▶ REFERENCE"},{"anchor":"command-cheatsheet","text":" Git 03 git checkout -b feat/x short branch, merge daily git push -u origin feat/x gh pr create --fill PR with CI gate See also: Git, Branching &amp; Pull Requests. 📘 Command Cheatsheet · By Nabawy — 3 — ▤ TOC Next REFERENCE npm / Builds 04 npm ci lockfile-exact install CI only npm test ./gradlew build wrapper pins toolchain See also: Build Systems. 📘 Command Cheatsheet · By Nabawy — 4 — ▤ TOC ◀ Prev · Next REFERENCE Docker / GHCR 05 docker build -t ghcr.io/OWNER/APP:sha-SHORT . docker push ghcr.io/OWNER/APP:sha-SHORT docker pull ghcr.io/OWNER/APP:sha-SHORT same digest everywhere See also: Artifact Management. Threads: Artifact Digest 📘 Command Cheatsheet · By Nabawy — 5 — ▤ TOC ◀ Prev · Next REFERENCE Jenkins Compose lab 06 docker compose up -d docker exec jenkins cat /var/jenkins_home/secrets/initialAdminPassword docker compose stop jenkins before backup docker compose down -v destroy everything See also: Lab 06, Lab 08. 📘 Command Cheatsheet · By Nabawy — 6 — ▤ TOC ◀ Prev · Next REFERENCE Jenkins backup 07 docker run --rm -v LAB_jenkins_home:/data -v \"$PWD\":/out alpine \\ tar czf /out/jenkins-backup.tar.gz -C /data . See also: Jenkins Advanced. 📘 Command Cheatsheet · By Nabaw"},{"anchor":"table-of-contents","text":"y — 7 — ▤ TOC ◀ Prev · Next REFERENCE GitHub Actions snippets 08 runs-on: ubuntu-latest needs: build DAG edge permissions: { packages: write } environment: production approval gate See also: GitHub Actions. Threads: Approval Gates 📘 Command Cheatsheet · By Nabawy — 8 — ▤ TOC ◀ Prev · Next REFERENCE Interview Notes 09 Reproduce any pipeline in this file from memory: trigger, build, gate, promote, rollback. Which snippet pins versions — and what breaks first when nothing is pinned. Where the approval gate lives in each platform's snippet, and what happens without it. Threads: Approval Gates 📘 Command Cheatsheet · By Nabawy — 9 — ▤ TOC ◀ Prev · Next REFERENCE Related Topics 10 Topic Index ../TOPIC_INDEX.md Glossary ../GLOSSARY.md 📘 Command Cheatsheet · By Nabawy — 10 — ▤ TOC ◀ Prev · ▲ Top ⏭️ Cycle back: Start Here: Foundations, Git &amp; CI Related books Delivery, Deployment &amp; Operations 255 min · Int → More to explore Jenkins Complete 345 min · Int → More to explore Labs Handbook 675 min · Int → More to explore Observability: Loki, Prometheus &amp; Grafana 120 min · Int → More to explore ↑ × Menu ← Back ⛶ Home ◐ Find in this book ◐ 0% ⛶ Exit ⛶"}]},"docker-18-contents-0-pages-0-sections":{"chunks":[{"anchor":"contents-0-pages-0-sections","text":"Lab 06: Jenkins Live — CICD BY Nabawy ☰ Contents CICD BY Nabawy / Lab 06: Jenkins Live ⌘K ☆ Save Overview ⚙ A⁺ CONTENTS · 0 PAGES · 0 SECTIONS ⭳ Open print HTML 🧪 Hands-on lab · 60 min · Env: Docker Compose · Overview &amp; prereqs · Reset checks Up next in Hands-on Labs → Lab 07: Shared Library Related books Lab 01: First Pipeline Lab 02: Build &amp; Test Lab 03: Artifacts Was this book useful? 👍 Yes 👎 No ← PREVIOUS Lab 05: Rollback NEXT → Lab 07: Shared Library Reading settings THEME Dark Light Sepia Dim Contrast FONT Sans Serif LINE HEIGHT Tight Comfort Roomy TEXT SIZE A− A A+ A++ READING WIDTH Narrow Comfortable Wide SHORTCUTS Ctrl+K search · T contents · ←/→ prev/next book · Esc close"}]},"docker-19-docker-cheat-sheet-complete-command-reference":{"chunks":[{"anchor":"docker-cheat-sheet-complete-command-reference","text":"{/ MIGRATED from: D:\\NTI Courses Master Folder\\Docker Master Folder\\docker-cheat-sheet.md Course metadata: course=docker reference 898 lines Note: source is a comprehensive CLI reference — migrated verbatim as the Docker track. Companion artifacts: docker-cheat-sheet.html + pdf, Day1 pdf/pptx, Lab2.pdf in source folder. /} Docker Cheat Sheet — Complete Command Reference A complete, up-to-date reference for the Docker CLI: containers, images, networks, volumes, registries, contexts, buildx/BuildKit, Compose v2, Swarm, Dockerfile, filtering/formatting, cleanup one-liners, and gotchas. --- Table of Contents Quick Start quick-start CLI Structure cli-structure Old vs New Command Syntax old-vs-new-command-syntax Containers containers Images images Registries registries Networks networks Volumes volumes System, Info & Cleanup system-info--cleanup Contexts contexts Build & Buildx BuildKit build--buildx-buildkit Docker Compose v2 docker-compose-v2 Swarm Mode swarm-mode Misc Commands misc-commands Dockerfile Reference dockerfile-reference .dockerignore dockerignore Filtering & Output Formatting filtering--output-formatting Useful One-Liners useful-one-liners Windows / PowerShell Notes window"},{"anchor":"table-of-contents","text":"s--powershell-notes Best Practices best-practices Security Checklist security-checklist Gotchas & Tips gotchas--tips --- Quick Start --- CLI Structure Modern Docker CLI uses management commands : Category Commands ------------- ---------- Container run , create , exec , attach , kill , pause , unpause , rename , update , wait , rm , ps , top , stats , port , diff , logs , export , commit , cp Image images , rmi , history , import , load , save , tag , search , pull , push , build Object mgmt container , image , network , volume , plugin , system , context , builder Build build , buildx , builder , manifest Registry login , logout , pull , push , search , trust , scout Compose compose Swarm swarm , node , service , stack , secret , config Misc info , version , events , inspect , checkpoint See source file for full sections: containers lifecycle and docker run flags, images lifecycle, registries, networks, volumes, system info, contexts, build/buildx, Compose v2, Swarm, Dockerfile reference, filtering, one-liners, Windows notes, best practices, security checklist, gotchas. Full source with all tables lives in docker-cheat-sheet.md in the source vault 898 lines . This page renders the"},{"anchor":"quick-start","text":" cheat sheet live; print via Ctrl+P → Save as PDF or use the companion docker-cheat-sheet.pdf for offline use. Key Sections summarized — full tables in source - Containers: create/run/start/stop/kill/pause/rm , all docker run flags -d , -p , -v , --mount , --network , --restart , --health-cmd , --init , resource limits, capabilities, rakes , restart policies, health checks, ps/logs/inspect/top/stats/port/diff/events , exec/cp/export/commit . - Images: images/pull/push/tag/rmi/history/inspect/prune/search , save/load/import/export , push workflow, digest pinning. - Networks: drivers bridge/host/none/overlay/macvlan , network create/ls/inspect/connect/disconnect/rm/prune , DNS on user-defined bridges. - Volumes: named vs bind vs tmpfs, volume create/ls/inspect/rm/prune , --mount syntax, backup/restore one-liners. - Build & Buildx: classic build + buildx multi-arch --platform , --push , --load , BuildKit cache/secret mounts. - Compose v2: up/down/ps/logs/exec/run/config/build/pull/push/watch , minimal compose.yaml example with healthcheck and depends_on . - Dockerfile: all instructions FROM/RUN/CMD/ENTRYPOINT/ENV/ARG/COPY/WORKDIR/USER/EXPOSE/VOLUME/LABEL/HEALTHCHECK , exec vs shell fo"},{"anchor":"cli-structure","text":"rm, multi-stage, slimming tips. - One-liners & Gotchas: batch ops, inspection, port maps, WSL2 notes, security checklist docker group = root, no sock exposure, non-root USER, cap-drop, read-only FS . Best Practices from source 1. One concern per container; treat containers as disposable. 2. Pin image versions/digests; never ship latest . 3. Multi-stage builds; smallest viable base. 4. Order layers cold→hot; leverage cache. 5. Non-root USER ; read-only filesystem where possible. 6. Externalize config via env vars; secrets via --secret /Swarm secrets. 7. Define HEALTHCHECK ; handle signals properly. 8. Named volumes for state; never bake data into images. 9. Use .dockerignore ; COPY only what's needed. 10. Scan images and refresh bases regularly. --- Source: Docker Master Folder/docker-cheat-sheet.md — 898 lines, companion HTML/PDF in same folder. Print this page or open the PDF for offline reference."}]},"docker-20-command-cheatsheet":{"chunks":[{"anchor":"command-cheatsheet","text":"Command Cheatsheet — CICD BY Nabawy SIGNAL SERIES · REFERENCE Command Cheatsheet Every command used across the labs and guides, grouped by tool. Copy-paste ready; details live in the linked chapters. By Nabawy AUTHOR &amp; COMPILER Git npm / Builds Docker / GHCR Jenkins Compose lab Jenkins backup V4 · 2026 · REFERENCE · verified 2026-09-18 Contents Table of Contents 8 sheets 03 Git 03 04 npm / Builds 04 05 Docker / GHCR 05 06 Jenkins Compose lab 06 07 Jenkins backup 07 08 GitHub Actions snippets 08 09 Interview Notes 09 10 Related Topics 10 How to use this book legend NOTE · context KEY · must retain WATCH OUT · gotcha VERIFIED · check NOTE Read cover to cover once, then use the Contents to jump. Every chapter ends with a 🎓 EXAM box — if you can answer it cold, move on. 📘 Command Cheatsheet · By Nabawy — 2 — Start ▶ REFERENCE Git 03 git checkout -b feat/x short branch, merge daily git push -u origin feat/x gh pr create --fill PR with CI gate See also: Git, Branching &amp; Pull Requests. 📘 Command Cheatsheet · By Nabawy — 3 — ▤ TOC Next REFERENCE npm / Builds 04 npm ci lockfile-exact install CI only npm test ./gradlew build wrapper pins toolchain See also: Build Systems. 📘 Comma"},{"anchor":"table-of-contents","text":"nd Cheatsheet · By Nabawy — 4 — ▤ TOC ◀ Prev · Next REFERENCE Docker / GHCR 05 docker build -t ghcr.io/OWNER/APP:sha-SHORT . docker push ghcr.io/OWNER/APP:sha-SHORT docker pull ghcr.io/OWNER/APP:sha-SHORT same digest everywhere See also: Artifact Management. Threads: Artifact Digest 📘 Command Cheatsheet · By Nabawy — 5 — ▤ TOC ◀ Prev · Next REFERENCE Jenkins Compose lab 06 docker compose up -d docker exec jenkins cat /var/jenkins_home/secrets/initialAdminPassword docker compose stop jenkins before backup docker compose down -v destroy everything See also: Lab 06, Lab 08. 📘 Command Cheatsheet · By Nabawy — 6 — ▤ TOC ◀ Prev · Next REFERENCE Jenkins backup 07 docker run --rm -v LAB_jenkins_home:/data -v \"$PWD\":/out alpine \\ tar czf /out/jenkins-backup.tar.gz -C /data . See also: Jenkins Advanced. 📘 Command Cheatsheet · By Nabawy — 7 — ▤ TOC ◀ Prev · Next REFERENCE GitHub Actions snippets 08 runs-on: ubuntu-latest needs: build DAG edge permissions: { packages: write } environment: production approval gate See also: GitHub Actions. Threads: Approval Gates 📘 Command Cheatsheet · By Nabawy — 8 — ▤ TOC ◀ Prev · Next REFERENCE Interview Notes 09 Reproduce any pipeline in this file from "},{"anchor":"git","text":"memory: trigger, build, gate, promote, rollback. Which snippet pins versions — and what breaks first when nothing is pinned. Where the approval gate lives in each platform's snippet, and what happens without it. Threads: Approval Gates 📘 Command Cheatsheet · By Nabawy — 9 — ▤ TOC ◀ Prev · Next REFERENCE Related Topics 10 Topic Index ../TOPIC_INDEX.md Glossary ../GLOSSARY.md 📘 Command Cheatsheet · By Nabawy — 10 — ▤ TOC ◀ Prev · ▲ Top ⏭️ Cycle back: Start Here: Foundations, Git &amp; CI"}]},"docker-21-lab-03-artifacts":{"chunks":[{"anchor":"lab-03-artifacts","text":"--- title: \"Lab 03 — Artifacts\" category: labs status: complete difficulty: intermediate prerequisites: - Lab 02 - Artifact Management related: - Artifact Management - CI/CD Security --- Lab 03 — Artifacts Objective Build a Docker image once, tag it by commit SHA, push to GHCR, attach an SBOM — then prove staging and prod would run the same digest. Prerequisites - Lab 02 green; a Dockerfile multi-stage, node:20-slim runtime ; concepts: Artifact Management ../05-artifacts-and-packaging/artifact-management.md . Architecture Merge to main → build image → tag ghcr.io/ / :sha- → push GHCR → generate SBOM → print digest the deployable identity . Setup Enable GitHub Packages GHCR on the repo; workflow needs packages: write permission. Step 1 — Build once, tag SHA Execute Expected Result GHCR shows sha- tag; logs print the digest. Why SHA tag = immutable identity; the digest not :latest is what every environment deploys see versioning ../05-artifacts-and-packaging/artifact-management.md . Step 2 — Attach SBOM Execute Add after push: anchore/sbom-action@v0 on the pushed image; inspect the artifact. Expected Result SBOM artifact lists every package inside the image. Why The ingredient list t"},{"anchor":"objective","text":"hat makes the next CVE answerable in minutes, not days. Verification - GHCR holds an immutable SHA tag. - Same digest referenced twice = same bytes. - SBOM artifact present. Failure Scenarios - 403 on push: missing packages: write — add the permission block. - latest deployed somewhere: retag nothing; fix consumers to pin the digest. Cleanup Keep images; they are small and prove immutability. What This Proved Build-once identity, SHA discipline, SBOM habit. Continue with Lab 04 04-deployment.md . Interview Notes - Digest vs tag: why redeploying latest can silently change what's running. - The 403 on push — which single permission block fixes it and why least privilege demands it."}]},"docker-22-lab-03-artifacts":{"chunks":[{"anchor":"lab-03-artifacts","text":"{/ MIGRATED from: Jenkins/node-multi-branch/16-labs/03-artifacts.md v2.0, 2026-09-01 Course metadata: course=cicd difficulty=Intermediate time=45min lab_env=Docker + GHCR prereqs=lab-02, artifacts Outcomes: tag by SHA; push to GHCR; generate SBOM Tags: lab, artifacts Path: labs /} {/ icon: artifact /} Lab 03 — Artifacts Objective Build a Docker image once, tag it by commit SHA, push to GHCR, attach an SBOM — then prove staging and prod would run the same digest. Prerequisites - Lab 02 green; a Dockerfile multi-stage, node:20-slim runtime ; concepts: Artifact Management /courses/cicd/artifacts/ . Architecture Merge to main → build image → tag ghcr.io/ / :sha- → push GHCR → generate SBOM → print digest the deployable identity . Setup Enable GitHub Packages GHCR on the repo; workflow needs packages: write permission. Step 1 — Build once, tag SHA Execute Expected Result GHCR shows sha- tag; logs print the digest. Why SHA tag = immutable identity; the digest not :latest is what every environment deploys see versioning /courses/cicd/artifacts/ . Step 2 — Attach SBOM Execute Add after push: anchore/sbom-action@v0 on the pushed image; inspect the artifact. Expected Result SBOM artifact lis"},{"anchor":"objective","text":"ts every package inside the image. Why The ingredient list that makes the next CVE answerable in minutes, not days. Verification - GHCR holds an immutable SHA tag. - Same digest referenced twice = same bytes. - SBOM artifact present. Failure Scenarios - 403 on push: missing packages: write — add the permission block. - latest deployed somewhere: retag nothing; fix consumers to pin the digest. Cleanup Keep images; they are small and prove immutability. What This Proved Build-once identity, SHA discipline, SBOM habit. Continue with Lab 04 /courses/cicd/lab-04/ ."}]},"docker-23-hands-on-br-labs":{"chunks":[{"anchor":"hands-onlabs","text":"CI/CD Engineering — Series S08: Hands-On Labs CI/CD SYSTEMS · SERIES EDITION · S08 HANDS-ON LABS Nine labs: first pipeline to backup drill. PDF 28: LAB 01 FIRST PIPELINE PDF 29: LAB 02 BUILD AND TEST PDF 30: LAB 03 ARTIFACTS PDF 31: LAB 04 DEPLOYMENT PDF 32: LAB 05 ROLLBACK + 4 MORE SERIES EDITION · 9 CHAPTERS Nabawy Series Edition &nbsp; S08 Contents Chapters PDF 28: Lab 01 First Pipeline 3 pages ↓ PDF 29: Lab 02 Build and Test 3 pages ↓ PDF 30: Lab 03 Artifacts 3 pages ↓ PDF 31: Lab 04 Deployment 3 pages ↓ PDF 32: Lab 05 Rollback 3 pages ↓ PDF 33: Lab 06 Jenkins Controller 3 pages ↓ PDF 34: Lab 07 Shared Library 3 pages ↓ PDF 35: Lab 08 Backup & Restore 3 pages ↓ PDF 36: Command Cheatsheet 3 pages ↓ OMNIBUS — member chapters keep their original page numbers. Print: Ctrl/Cmd+P → Save as PDF → Background graphics on → A4. CI/CD Engineering Manual S08 02 / 29 CI/CD SYSTEMS &nbsp;·&nbsp; LABS &nbsp;·&nbsp; PDF 28 LAB 01 FIRST PIPELINE Push → build → test → green check GITHUB ACTIONS · HOSTED RUNNER STATUS CHECK · BRANCH PROTECTION 01 / 03 · THE SMALLEST CLOSED LOOP Nabawy Labs &nbsp; Lab 01 Execute Workflow · Gate 01 / Add the Workflow .github/workflows/ci.yml name: ci on: { push, pu"},{"anchor":"contents","text":"ll_request } jobs: build: runs-on: ubuntu-latest steps: - uses: actions/checkout@v4 - uses: actions/setup-node@v4 with: { node-version: 20, cache: npm } - run: npm ci - run: npm test EXPECT — green run in Actions tab, green check on the PR. Push to a branch, open a PR. Trigger → runner → job → feedback: the smallest closed loop. 02 / Require the Check EXECUTE — Settings → Branches → protect main → require build . Push a breaking change: the merge button blocks until green. An unenforced gate is decoration. CI/CD Engineering Manual LAB 01 02 / 03 Labs &nbsp; Lab 01 Verify Checks · Next 03 / Verification PR shows the green workflow check. Merging without green is blocked. Breaking the test turns the PR red. Trouble: npm ci fails → commit the lockfile; long queues → shrink the job. Proved: triggers fire pipelines, runners execute jobs, checks gate merges. Next: Lab 02 · Build and Test . SOURCE: 16-labs/01-first-pipeline.md · Print: Ctrl/Cmd+P → Save as PDF → Background graphics on → A4. CI/CD Engineering Manual LAB 01 03 / 03 CI/CD SYSTEMS &nbsp;·&nbsp; LABS &nbsp;·&nbsp; PDF 29 LAB 02 BUILD + TEST DAG jobs, cached deps, hermetic proof NEEDS EDGES · LOCKFILE CACHE LOCAL == CI · UNDER "},{"anchor":"lab-01first-pipeline","text":"10 MIN 01 / 03 · FAST, SEALED, ORDERED Nabawy Labs &nbsp; Lab 02 Execute Split · Cache 01 / Split and Cache jobs: build: npm ci, cached on lockfile runs-on: ubuntu-latest steps: ... test: needs: build the DAG edge runs-on: ubuntu-latest steps: ..., npm test EXPECT — two ordered jobs; reruns faster on warm npm cache. needs: is the ordering edge; the lockfile-keyed cache is the hermetic speedup. 02 / Prove Hermeticity EXECUTE — delete node_modules , run npm ci &amp;&amp; npm test locally. Identical pass/fail as CI: the runner builds what you built, no ambient-machine surprises. CI/CD Engineering Manual LAB 02 02 / 03 Labs &nbsp; Lab 02 Verify Checks · Next 03 / Verification Two jobs visible, ordered by needs . Cache hit on rerun; local npm ci reproduces CI. Trouble: cache never hits → lockfile untracked; wrong order → missing needs: . Proved: DAG ordering, hermetic installs, lockfile caching. Next: Lab 03 · Artifacts . SOURCE: 16-labs/02-build-and-test.md · Print: Ctrl/Cmd+P → Save as PDF → Background graphics on → A4. CI/CD Engineering Manual LAB 02 03 / 03 CI/CD SYSTEMS &nbsp;·&nbsp; LABS &nbsp;·&nbsp; PDF 30 LAB 03 ARTIFACTS Build once — tag SHA, push, SBOM GHCR · IMMUTABLE TAGS S"},{"anchor":"execute","text":"BOM · DIGEST IDENTITY 01 / 03 · ONE BUILD, EVERY ENVIRONMENT Nabawy Labs &nbsp; Lab 03 Execute Image · SBOM 01 / Build Once, Tag SHA - uses: docker/login-action@v3 with: { registry: ghcr.io, ... } - uses: docker/metadata-action@v5 with: images: ghcr.io/OWNER/APP tags: type=sha,prefix=sha- - uses: docker/build-push-action@v6 with: { push: true } EXPECT — GHCR shows sha-&lt;short&gt; ; logs print the digest. SHA = immutable identity; the digest is what every environment deploys — never :latest . 02 / Attach SBOM EXECUTE — add anchore/sbom-action@v0 after push. The ingredient list that makes the next CVE answerable in minutes. CI/CD Engineering Manual LAB 03 02 / 03 Labs &nbsp; Lab 03 Verify Checks · Next 03 / Verification GHCR holds an immutable SHA tag; same digest = same bytes. SBOM artifact present. Trouble: 403 on push → add packages: write . Proved: build-once identity, SHA discipline, SBOM habit. Next: Lab 04 · Deployment . SOURCE: 16-labs/03-artifacts.md · Print: Ctrl/Cmd+P → Save as PDF → Background graphics on → A4. CI/CD Engineering Manual LAB 03 03 / 03 CI/CD SYSTEMS &nbsp;·&nbsp; LABS &nbsp;·&nbsp; PDF 31 LAB 04 DEPLOYMENT Same digest, two environments, one gate STAGING A"},{"anchor":"01-add-the-workflow","text":"UTO · PROD APPROVAL DIGEST PROMOTION 01 / 03 · RELEASABLE ALWAYS, DEPLOYED BY DECISION Nabawy Labs &nbsp; Lab 04 Execute Staging · Gate 01 / Staging Auto-Deploys deploy-staging: needs: image environment: staging runs-on: ubuntu-latest steps: - run: echo \"deploy DIGEST to staging\" EXPECT — every main merge deploys staging, no clicks. Safe to automate: users never see it. 02 / Production Needs a Human EXECUTE — add deploy-prod with environment: production required reviewer in Settings → Environments . Production waits; after approval it runs the same digest . This is Continuous Delivery's one gate. CI/CD Engineering Manual LAB 04 02 / 03 Labs &nbsp; Lab 04 Verify Checks · Next 03 / Verification Staging deploys on every merge; production blocks until approved. Both ran the identical digest — pass digests explicitly, never tags. Proved: promotion paths, environment gates, digest discipline. Next: Lab 05 · Rollback — production breaks on purpose. SOURCE: 16-labs/04-deployment.md · Print: Ctrl/Cmd+P → Save as PDF → Background graphics on → A4. CI/CD Engineering Manual LAB 04 03 / 03 CI/CD SYSTEMS &nbsp;·&nbsp; LABS &nbsp;·&nbsp; PDF 32 LAB 05 ROLLBACK Break production — recover in minute"},{"anchor":"expect-green-run-in-actions-tab-green-check-on-the-pr","text":"s OLD DIGESTS · TIMED RECOVERY POSTMORTEM WITH TEETH 01 / 03 · RECOVERY, PRACTICED Nabawy Labs &nbsp; Lab 05 Execute Incident · Recover 01 / Cause the Incident EXECUTE — merge a change that passes tests but breaks visible output wrong port, broken route . Promote to production. Green ≠ correct. This is why recovery is engineered, not improvised. 02 / Roll Back, Don't Rebuild EXECUTE — redeploy the previous digest re-run old deploy-prod . Time yourself. Old digests are proven in production; fresh builds add untested variables to a fire. 03 / Postmortem With Teeth WRITE — timeline + root cause + one action item that becomes a merged pipeline change new test, check, or alert . Postmortems without merged changes are diary entries. CI/CD Engineering Manual LAB 05 02 / 03 Labs &nbsp; Lab 05 Verify Checks · Next 04 / Verification Recovery time measured — aim minutes. Previous digest redeployed, not a fresh build. One preventive pipeline change merged; postmortem filed in docs/postmortems/ . Proved: recovery as a practiced path. General labs complete — next: Lab 06 · Jenkins Controller . SOURCE: 16-labs/05-rollback.md · Print: Ctrl/Cmd+P → Save as PDF → Background graphics on → A4. CI/CD E"},{"anchor":"02-require-the-check","text":"ngineering Manual LAB 05 03 / 03 CI/CD SYSTEMS &nbsp;·&nbsp; LABS &nbsp;·&nbsp; PDF 33 LAB 06 JENKINS LIVE Controller + agent in Compose, first pipeline DOCKER COMPOSE · INBOUND AGENT THREE GREEN STAGES 01 / 03 · SCHEDULE HERE, EXECUTE THERE Nabawy Labs &nbsp; Lab 06 Execute Agent · Pipeline 01 / Connect the Agent docker compose up -d docker exec jenkins cat \\ /var/jenkins_home/secrets/initialAdminPassword EXPECT — node agent-1 online, label docker-agent , 2–4 executors. Create the node in Manage Jenkins → Nodes, launch inbound with the secret. Controller schedules, agents execute. 02 / Run the Canonical Pipeline pipeline { agent { label 'docker-agent' } stages { stage 'Build' { steps { sh 'echo build' } } stage 'Test' { steps { sh 'echo test' } } stage 'Deploy' { steps { echo 'deploy' } } } } EXPECT — three green boxes in Stage View, all on agent-1 . Controller runs zero builds — no executors on master , ever, in prod. CI/CD Engineering Manual LAB 06 02 / 03 Labs &nbsp; Lab 06 Verify Checks · Next 03 / Verification Agent online, labeled, idle after the run. Three green stages; controller executors at zero. Trouble: agent offline → secret/URL wrong; use http://jenkins:8080 in-Compo"},{"anchor":"execute-settings-branches-protect-main-require-build","text":"se. Proved: the controller/agent split hands-on. Next: Lab 07 · Shared Library . SOURCE: 16-labs/06-jenkins-controller.md · Print: Ctrl/Cmd+P → Save as PDF → Background graphics on → A4. CI/CD Engineering Manual LAB 06 03 / 03 CI/CD SYSTEMS &nbsp;·&nbsp; LABS &nbsp;·&nbsp; PDF 34 LAB 07 SHARED LIBRARY One step defined — two pipelines consuming VARS/ · VERSION PIN DRY WITH A SAFETY RAIL 01 / 03 · REUSE WITHOUT COPY-PASTE Nabawy Labs &nbsp; Lab 07 Execute Register · Consume 01 / Register the Library pipeline-library repo, vars/standardTest.groovy def call Map cfg = : { sh \"${cfg.cmd ?: 'npm test'}\" } EXECUTE — Manage Jenkins → System → Global Pipeline Libraries → add my-lib , version main . Versioned libraries are dependencies: pinned, reviewed, rollback-able. 02 / Consume From Two Pipelines @Library 'my-lib' _ pipeline { agent { label 'docker-agent' } stages { stage 'Test' { steps { standardTest cmd: 'npm test' } } } } EXPECT — both pipelines green on the shared step. One definition, N consumers — the DRY payoff, version pin as safety rail. CI/CD Engineering Manual LAB 07 02 / 03 Labs &nbsp; Lab 07 Verify Checks · Next 03 / Verification Breaking the library breaks both proves sharin"},{"anchor":"verify","text":"g ; reverting fixes both. Library changes go through PR review on their own repo. Trouble: not found → name/version mismatch; sandbox blocks → approve or restrict source. Proved: versioned pipeline reuse. Next: Lab 08 · Backup &amp; Restore . SOURCE: 16-labs/07-jenkins-shared-library.md · Print: Ctrl/Cmd+P → Save as PDF → Background graphics on → A4. CI/CD Engineering Manual LAB 07 03 / 03 CI/CD SYSTEMS &nbsp;·&nbsp; LABS &nbsp;·&nbsp; PDF 35 LAB 08 BACKUP + RESTORE Destroy the controller — bring it back OFFLINE SNAPSHOT · FULL WIPE RESTORE · SMOKE REPLAY 01 / 03 · RECOVERY MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED Nabawy Labs &nbsp; Lab 08 Execute Backup · Destroy 01 / Back Up Offline docker compose stop jenkins docker run --rm \\ -v jenkins-lab_jenkins_home:/data \\ -v \"$PWD\":/out alpine \\ tar czf /out/jenkins-backup.tar.gz -C /data . docker compose start jenkins WHY OFFLINE — stopped controller = consistent snapshot; live copies risk half-written state. Record one job's build number + one credential ID — the restore proof. 02 / Destroy and Restore docker compose down -v everything, incl. volume docker compose up -d fresh controller docker compose stop jenkins docker run --rm \\ -v jenkins-lab_jenkins_"},{"anchor":"03-verification","text":"home:/data \\ -v \"$PWD\":/in alpine \\ tar xzf /in/jenkins-backup.tar.gz -C /data docker compose start jenkins CI/CD Engineering Manual LAB 08 02 / 03 Labs &nbsp; Lab 08 Verify Checks · Done 03 / Verification Jobs, history, credentials, plugins all present — recorded build number matches. Lab 06's smoke pipeline runs green on the restored controller. Trouble: plugin drift → pin image digests; UID errors → chown -R 1000:1000 the volume. Proved: disaster recovery measured against reality. Labs complete — return to the Topic Index . SOURCE: 16-labs/08-jenkins-backup-restore.md · Print: Ctrl/Cmd+P → Save as PDF → Background graphics on → A4. CI/CD Engineering Manual LAB 08 03 / 03 CI/CD SYSTEMS &nbsp;·&nbsp; REFERENCE &nbsp;·&nbsp; PDF 36 COMMAND CHEATSHEET Every lab command, grouped by tool GIT · NPM · DOCKER JENKINS · ACTIONS SNIPPETS 01 / 03 · COPY-PASTE READY Nabawy Reference &nbsp; Cheatsheet Commands Git · Build · Docker 01 / Git + npm git checkout -b feat/x git push -u origin feat/x gh pr create --fill npm ci lockfile-exact, CI only npm test ./gradlew build wrapper pins toolchain 02 / Docker / GHCR docker build -t ghcr.io/OWNER/APP:sha-SHORT . docker push ghcr.io/OWNER/APP:sha-SHOR"},{"anchor":"lab-02build-test","text":"T docker pull ghcr.io/OWNER/APP:sha-SHORT 03 / Jenkins Compose docker compose up -d docker exec jenkins cat /var/jenkins_home/secrets/initialAdminPassword docker compose stop jenkins before backup docker compose down -v destroy everything CI/CD Engineering Manual REF 02 / 03 Reference &nbsp; Cheatsheet Snippets Backup · Actions 04 / Jenkins Backup docker run --rm -v LAB_jenkins_home:/data \\ -v \"$PWD\":/out alpine \\ tar czf /out/jenkins-backup.tar.gz -C /data . 05 / Actions Snippets runs-on: ubuntu-latest needs: build DAG edge permissions: { packages: write } environment: production approval gate Full context lives in the linked chapters — this sheet is the memory aid, not the manual. SOURCE: 17-reference/command-cheatsheet.md · Print: Ctrl/Cmd+P → Save as PDF → Background graphics on → A4. CI/CD Engineering Manual REF 03 / 03"}]}});